{{!-- The archive grid: the rest of the shared window, as cards. Both homepage layouts draw it.

	{{> "home/archive-grid" feed=feed start="6" mt="mt-14"}}         A1, beneath the lead band
	{{> "home/archive-grid" feed=feed start="2"}}                    A1, in place of it
	{{> "home/archive-grid" feed=../../feed start="6" narrow=true}}  A2's imageless grid

	feed     the shared post window from home/window.hbs
	start    the window position the grid opens on — 6 below a lead band, 2 without one
	mt       the gap above, as a class: the band's 56px, or nothing when the section
	         heading's own 32px is the gap (home/latest.hbs decides which)
	narrow   the track arithmetic for A2's 1120px article column rather than A1's page width

A2 CALLS THIS PARTIAL RATHER THAN CARRYING A CARD OF ITS OWN. Its own grid is photographs — two
tracks of 5/4 images with no excerpt, the image doing the work — and a publication with no
photographs left that grid drawing title-and-date over 500px of nothing. The card that answers
is the one already written here, so the two layouts' imageless grids are one partial and not two
drawings of the same idea. What A2 keeps is the rule above the grid: home/digest.hbs wraps this
call, because that rule closes the tag strip above it rather than opening the grid.

`start` to `to="14"`, taken with {{#foreach}}'s 1-based inclusive `from`/`to`. Slicing rather than
excluding is deliberate: core/frontend/helpers/get.js only optimises a filter carrying exactly one
`id:-` term, so a chain of exclusions would make the most-requested URL on the site uncacheable.

The slice moves because the lead band above it is not always drawn — see home/latest.hbs for the
condition and docs/adr/0005 for why the posts have to land somewhere rather than be dropped.

THE TRACK COUNT FOLLOWS THE CARDS, AND THAT IS NOT `auto-fill`. Nine cards over three tracks fill
every row, which is why the window is nine here; `auto-fill` derives the count from the WIDTH
(four at this page's 1256px content box), so seven cards drew 4 + 3 and left an empty cell. But
the slice is only nine cards on a publication that HAS fourteen posts: at seven posts it is two,
and two cards in three fixed tracks left a third of the row empty — an **Underset**
(CONTEXT.md).

`auto-fit` collapses a track only when that track is empty in EVERY row, so a partly-filled last
row — five author cards over three tracks — is untouched, and the keyword changes nothing for any
grid whose item count reaches its track count. `min(360px,100%)` is what pins the count: 1256px of
content box divided by a 360px track plus a 36px gap derives three tracks and not four, which is
the arithmetic `auto-fill` got wrong, and the design's own 394px card is the result. Two cards
then take two tracks instead of leaving a hole, and the demo homepage is byte-identical at the
1568px capture width.

`*:max-w-[480px]` caps what a collapsed track may do to a card. Without it one card in one track
sets 1256px wide — larger than the lead story — and with it a sparse row keeps cards at card
scale and simply ends early. 480px is above the drawn 394px, so it is inert whenever the grid is
full. It is the cap for both columns: A2's derived card is 328px.

`narrow` IS THE SAME ARITHMETIC OVER A DIFFERENT COLUMN, which is why both numbers live here
rather than being passed in as a class. A2 narrows its article column to 1120px (home.hbs), and
`container` takes 2rem off each side, so the content box is 1056px: a 320px track plus a 36px gap
divides it 3.07 times, deriving three tracks of 328px — the width the design source draws for
that column. 360px would derive 2.75 and round down to two, and anything at or below 237px would
derive four. Between a 1024px and a 1184px viewport the column is the viewport rather than
1120px, so both grids fall to two tracks there; that is the arithmetic, not a special case.

`lg`, not `stack`, is where the tracks start, and the number is the audit's: a card's paragraph
has to clear 250px (`scripts/verify/probe-audit.js`), and three tracks plus two 36px gaps inside a
768px viewport leave 211px each — eight NARROW failures, measured. The design source has the
ragged row instead (`homepage-01.astro` slices seven into an `auto-fill` grid); this is a
divergence, recorded in DECISIONS.md. --}}
<div class="{{mt}} grid grid-cols-1 gap-9 *:max-w-[480px] {{#if narrow}}lg:grid-cols-[repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(320px,100%),1fr))]{{else}}lg:grid-cols-[repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(360px,100%),1fr))]{{/if}}">
	{{#foreach feed from=start to="14"}}
		{{!-- The rule closes an imageless card rather than capping it. A photographed card is
		capped by its own image and the design draws no rule on it; an imageless one has to say
		where it ends, and the rule reads better at the foot than at the head because the section
		heading's own `border-b` already caps the first row — a top rule put a second hairline
		28px under it and left the last row open. Same direction as the companion rows beside a
		photographed lead, which have always used `border-b`. --}}
		<article class="{{post_class}} {{#unless feature_image}}border-border border-b pb-6{{/unless}}">
			<a
				href="{{url}}"
				class="text-text block no-underline">
				{{#if feature_image}}
					<img
						src="{{img_url feature_image size="m"}}"
						{{> "image-srcset" image=feature_image sizes="(min-width: 768px) 480px, 100vw"}}
						{{#if feature_image_alt}}alt="{{feature_image_alt}}"{{else}}alt=""{{/if}}
						loading="lazy"
						decoding="async"
						class="border-border block aspect-[3/2] w-full rounded-md border object-cover">
				{{/if}}

				<div class="mt-4 flex items-center gap-2.5">
					{{#primary_tag}}
						<span class="font-meta text-meta tracking-meta text-accent uppercase">{{name}}</span>
					{{/primary_tag}}

					{{> "tier-chip"}}
				</div>

				<h3 class="font-display tracking-heading mt-2.5 text-[22px] leading-[1.24] font-medium">{{title}}</h3>

				<p class="text-small text-muted mt-2.5 leading-[1.58]">{{excerpt}}</p>

				<p class="font-meta text-meta tracking-meta text-muted mt-3.5 uppercase">{{> "dateline"}}</p>
			</a>
		</article>
	{{/foreach}}
</div>
